References: <VLIXz5i3vZI.lWVNpRnr930J@davis-rug.groups.google.com> <VLIXz5i3vZI.0vFlaK-OMNsJ@davis-rug.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/davis-rug/c/VLIXz5i3vZI/m/9SeAXLSoMCcJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

This is because there is overhead in creating threads (or forking processes), setting up and copying over over data into thread stacks, etc. Consider this a high fixed cost that only washes out in the long run of a computationally expensive task. If your task is short, there is no benefit to parallelization. There's even more wall-clock time loss in some cases if you've spent hours parallelizing your code!

Premature optimization (and parallelization) are the root of all evil (http://c2.com/cgi/wiki?PrematureOptimization). I wouldn't recommend parallelizing a task until you've profiled it using Rprof. The exception of course is if it is for learning — but even in this case, I would recommend also using Rprof too.

HTH,
Vince

